          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true. Its place in an elaborate comparative declaration reinforces its avowal force, regardless of whether the profession is sincere or exaggerated.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows present love but does not promise future conduct, assume a new obligation, or specify a relational undertaking. Lear solicits a declaration of who loves him most, not a promise; his ensuing grant is a response to that declaration rather than acceptance of an undertaking by Goneril.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs in a deliberate, rhetorically developed answer to Lear's formal solicitation. Nothing supplied indicates a reflex-like affective discharge beyond the avowal itself; the claim that words are inadequate is part of the assertion's rhetoric, not evidence of involuntary verbal release.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.82,\"reason\":\"Besides avowing love, the expression functions within a competitive answer intended to establish Goneril's merit in Lear's love test and thereby support her claim to his bounty. This strategic, flattering answer-function is contextually substantial but secondary to the focal expression's direct avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to state which loves him most and explicitly links their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Speaking first, Goneril addresses Lear with the focal avowal as the opening of an elaborate claim that her love exceeds ordinary comparison. Lear then awards her territory.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril makes no definite promise or new relational undertaking in the focal expression. Lear treats her declaration as a successful answer in the love-and-bounty proceeding and grants her territory, but that is uptake of her professed comparative love, not acceptance of a specified commitment. The passage supplies a direct profession of affection while its competitive, reward-linked setting and Cordelia's contrasting comments give reason for caution about sincerity or degree.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage is recognised as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear. The assessment relies only on the supplied text and does not use later plot events.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene establishes the public, competitive setting and the linguistic force of the focal expression, but it does not independently settle Goneril's actual feelings, private intentions, or whether any affection she has matches the extreme degree she professes.\"}"
        }
